    "version": "0.1.0",
    "chemistry": "rna004",
    "notes": "Successor family to barcode_crf_nbc16_rna004, closed at v0.3.1 (nbc -> ldx rename; same panel, corpus lineage and architecture \u2014 only the name and the reported class names change). The history below is that lineage's.\nFirst model trained without bonito or ont-koi. The two stacks are EQUIVALENT, established over 3 paired seeds rather than one: same corpus, same recorded split, same seed per pair, so the per-seed difference is the signal and its spread is the error bar (benchmarks/crf_bonito_free/ab_3seed.json, scripts/ldx/analysis/ab_seeds.py). Every metric SIGN-FLIPS across seeds \u2014 exact match -0.03/+0.03/+1.28pp, balanced recall +0.13/-0.10/+0.44pp \u2014 so the means are not effects. A single seed had shown every metric moving the right way by ~0.4pp; three seeds show that was the ~1 point run-to-run spread this task is known for, which is exactly the ordering that spread invents. The three replaced pieces are separately verified against bonito on real weights: encoder bit-exact, loss to 1.9e-06 with gradients to 6e-08, decode rule exact (our pass 2 on bonito's own posteriors reproduces its strings on 512/512 reads).\nRetrained at the corrected geometry (issue #36). state_len=4 means the CRF emits target[4:]; the 48-nt target puts those four sacrificial bases in the oligo's constant prefix so the full 27-nt code is emitted, where the previous 40-nt target discarded the code's first four bases and discriminated on 23 nt. Measured accuracy-neutral at 16-plex across 4 geometries x 3 seeds \u2014 the 16-plex is not discrimination-limited \u2014 but it restores the separability the 96-code pool was designed for, which an 80-plex needs. Match decodes against the EMITTED references shipped in the bundle, never the full-length targets. The pilot is CONFOUNDED (each ldx code on exactly one flowcell): report two 8x8 matrices, never one 16x16."
  },
